A number formatter that called a method nobody had.

Swift Publisher's Document Margins fields ignored every keystroke. The click was landing on the right
view (CIDER_HIT says NSTextField frame {{76, 73}, {57, 19}} responder=1), a field editor was being set
up (CIDER_FIELDEDIT cell=NSTextFieldCell string=9 editable=1) and the editor took the mouseDown, and
still nothing typed. The log said why by its SIZE: 1.87 million lines against 16 thousand for a run
that types into a field that works.

    68612  cider: UNRECOGNIZED -[CCMeasurementUnitsFormatter getObjectValue:forString:range:error:]
    68612    chain CCNumberFormatter   ...   NSNumberFormatter   ...   NSFormatter
   137224  CIDER_EXC AppKit -[NSView _displayIfNeededWithoutViewWillDraw]

-[NSNumberFormatter getObjectValue:forString:errorDescription:] here forwards to
getObjectValue:forString:range:error:, and NOTHING IN THE CHAIN IMPLEMENTED THAT: not this class, and
NSFormatter had no default for it either. So every parse raised, AppKit caught the raise per event and
the field editor tried again on the next display pass, and the event loop never got far enough to
process a key. The application ships on macOS, where both methods exist.

Two methods, then. The concrete parse belongs to NSNumberFormatter and goes through the CFNumberFormatter
the class already uses for numberFromString:, honouring the range it is given. The other is the
documented default on NSFormatter: a subclass that implements only the errorDescription: form still has
to answer the range: form, so the base class bridges to it and wraps the description in an NSError.

AND THE RANGE ITSELF WAS UNINITIALISED. The existing errorDescription: implementation declared NSRange r
and passed &r without setting it, which nobody noticed while the callee did not exist. With the callee
in place, CoreFoundation read whatever the stack held and faulted inside CFNumberFormatterGetValueFromString.
It is set to the whole string now, and the concrete method clamps any range a caller passes rather than
trusting it.

Measured after: the field takes 30 and shows it with a caret, four key events are delivered where zero
were before, zero raises, and the log is 15,724 lines.
